A||N||4||Vlad||Bad IAC or not?||||5||
Z||000000||Vlad||02-05-2001||03:20 PM||||Hello,<P>A couple of days ago I was playing with some sensors and what I suddenly  realised, that if I disconnect IAC while the car is idling - nothing happens. <BR>THe idle is still solid and CHECK ENGINE light does not light up.<P>Even though I've never had a problem with idle speed, I have a reason to think, that my IAC is bad. <BR>Would that no CHECK ENGINE light situation support bad IAC version?<BR>I mean if this normal for computer not to respond to disconnected IAC?<P>Thanks,<P>85 GT ||||reg||5
Z||000001||LarryB||02-05-2001||04:58 PM||||I know the test for a 4 cyl is to shut off the engine, unplug the IAC, then restart the car.  If the IAC is working correctly the engine will idle at about 3000 RPM. (tried this when looking for the cause of a code 35 which turned out to be a small vacuum leak.)<P>I don't have my Helms handy right now but I'd assume the same test for a V6 would be valid?||||reg||5
Z||000002||Rodrv6||02-05-2001||05:46 PM||||If you are getting a fast idle when the engine is cold and it drops to a normal idle as it warms up, then the IAC is working.  Disconnecting it while the engine is idling may not change the idle speed.  I would be careful about disconnecting any of the fuel injection components while the engine is running--a voltage spike won't do the computer any good at all.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-05-2001||05:47 PM||||The computer does not have feedback from the IAC. If the car is idling and you unplug it the ECM won't know as long as Idle RPM stays where the ECM wants it. If the ECM tryed to change the idle then it might set a code. Depends how many steps the ECM tried to move the IAC.<P>You don't want to keep doing that. you could damage the ECM. The ECM often has power on one or the other IAC coil even when it's not moving. Holding power on one coil makes sure the IAC pintle can't drift due to vibration.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Vlad||02-05-2001||06:10 PM||||TheOgre and guys,<P>Vibration, vibration, vibration.........<P>That's the problem. Sometimes, when I slow down to complete stop, there is a vibration (sort of zooming, very loud) coming from throttle body. I even can feel it in gas pedal. Once you step on the gas - it's gone.<P>I took off throttle body for cleaning and inspection. Butterfly is fine and cables are ok. The only thing that is left is IAC. <BR>I have not taken it out yet, but I'm afraid I will found  IAC in pieces. <P>Otherwise, I have no idle problems of any kind. <P>Advice?<P>||||reg||1
